  "slug": "resolving-significance-5c78",
  "summary": "Recent preprints and publications apply learning models to cfDNA fragmentomic and single-cell readouts for cancer detection and cell annotation.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is inferring cell or tumor properties from indirect, sample-level, or noisy measurements using machine learning. TuFEst applies ML to genome-wide cfDNA fragmentomics for early breast cancer detection, molecular subtyping and lymph node assessment. The preprint on per-base quality scores in cfDNA WGS extends this fragmentomic framing: the scores carry fragmentomic signal, and a leave-one-batch-out classifier reaches pooled AUC 0.81 for cancer. Other items address single-cell data under weaker or constrained supervision: scMILD learns condition-associated cells from sample-level labels, scEvolver accumulates cell-type prototypes without revisiting historical data, and NIFty handles missing data in single-cell proteomics while avoiding double dipping. What is new is the input: per-base quality scores from cfDNA WGS.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-03-12"
}
